    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "retenir",
     "title": "Changes of state",
     "md": "- **Melting**: solid to liquid (ice melts at 0 °C).\n- **Freezing**: liquid to solid.\n- **Evaporation**: liquid to gas at the surface at any temperature.\n- **Boiling**: liquid to gas throughout, at the boiling point (water 100 °C at sea level).\n- **Condensation**: gas to liquid.\n- **Sublimation**: solid straight to gas, e.g. iodine.\n\nThese are **physical changes**: no new substance is made."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Gases have mass: a football full of air weighs more than an empty one.\n- Temperature stays constant while a pure substance melts or boils.\n- Evaporation is not boiling: clothes dry on a line well below 100 °C."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Name the change",
     "statement": "A block of ice in a Douala market is left in the sun and becomes water. Later the puddle disappears. Name each change.",
     "steps": [
      "Ice (solid) to water (liquid) is melting.",
      "Water disappearing into the air is evaporation."
     ],
     "answer": "Melting, then evaporation."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Reading a heating record",
     "statement": "A beaker of water is heated and its temperature recorded each minute: 20, 40, 60, 80, 100, 100, 100 °C. What is the boiling point and how do you know?",
     "steps": [
      "The temperature rises and then stays constant.",
      "It stays at 100 °C while the water boils."
     ],
     "answer": "100 °C"
    },
    {
